\section{Addressing a row}{

\code{term} matches, in this order, the \code{term} column, the \code{label} column,
and a term with the brms class prefix removed (\code{"wt"} finds \code{b_wt}).
For a hypotheses table it matches \code{hypothesis}. A structural-equation
path is addressed by its two sides: \code{apa_inline(x, "visual", "x1", op = "=~")}; the order of the sides does not matter for a covariance
(\verb{~~}) and does for every other operator. \code{op} alone finds an
intercept (\code{op = "~1"}). \code{group} restricts the search first, for a
multi-group fit or a grouped hypothesis. \code{term = NULL} reports every
row. No match, or more than one, is an error that lists the
candidates.
}

\section{What is printed}{

A \code{parameters} row prints the estimate, its interval labelled by the
row's own \code{ci_method} (\code{CrI} for an equal-tailed credible interval,
\code{HDI}, \code{CI} for a Wald or bootstrap interval), then whichever of the
probability of direction, the ROPE share, the Bayes factor and the p
value the row carries. A population-level regression coefficient
(\code{component} \code{"conditional"}, not a random-effect term) is prefixed
\verb{*b*}; other rows carry no symbol unless \code{symbol} gives one. A
standardized row (\code{std}) drops the leading zero. A \code{hypotheses} row
prints the estimate, the interval at that row's level and the Bayes
factor; \code{stats} can add the evidence ratio and the posterior
probability. A \code{diagnostics} row prints R-hat and both effective
sample sizes.

A \code{sem_fit} row from \code{\link[=apa_tidy_sem_fit]{apa_tidy_sem_fit()}} prints the indices it
carries, in a fixed order: for a lavaan fit \verb{χ²(df) = …, *p* …, CFI, TLI, RMSEA with its confidence interval, SRMR}; for a blavaan fit
\verb{PPP, BRMSEA and BΓ̂} with their credible intervals, labelled by the
table's \code{ci_method}. The indices print with three decimals and no
leading zero and χ² with two, unless \code{digits} sets both; \code{stats}
selects among \code{"chisq"} (with its \emph{p}), \code{"cfi"}, \code{"tli"},
\code{"rmsea"}, \code{"srmr"}, \code{"ppp"}, \code{"brmsea"} and \code{"bgammahat"}. The
default method extracts with \code{\link[=apa_tidy]{apa_tidy()}}, which is the parameter
table, so fit indices are reported from \code{apa_tidy_sem_fit(fit)}.

A \code{loo} row from a \code{\link[loo:loo_compare]{loo::loo_compare()}} table prints the difference
in expected log predictive density to the best model with its
standard error, \verb{ΔELPD = −0.97, *SE* = 0.35}, on every row, the best
model's \code{0.00} included; \code{stats} adds \code{"elpd"} (the model's own ELPD
and SE), \code{"p_loo"}, \code{"looic"} and \code{"weight"} (\verb{*w*}, when the table
was extracted with \verb{weights =}; which kind of weight it is lives in
the table's \code{weight_method} attribute, not in the string). A
\code{bf_models} row prints its Bayes factor against the table's
denominator model, \verb{*BF*~10~ = 6.38} (the denominator itself prints
\code{1.00}); \code{stats} adds \code{"log_bf"} and \code{"post_prob"}, the posterior
model probability under equal prior odds, \verb{*P*(M | D)}. A Bayes
factor too large or too small to exponentiate (a log Bayes factor
beyond about ±709) prints as its log instead, never as \verb{∞} or \code{0}.
Such a row is addressed by \code{model}, and a Bayes-factor row also by
the \code{name} it was passed as. LOO and Bayes-factor comparisons answer
different questions and are never merged into one string.

A \code{contrasts} row from an emmeans grid prints like a parameters row:
the estimate, its interval labelled from the row's \code{ci_method}
(\verb{95\% HPD [1.38, 7.01]} on the emmGrid route's default, \code{CrI} under
